# SU(1)

## NAME

su - substitute user id temporarily

## SYNOPSIS

| **su** [ userid ]

## DESCRIPTION

*Su* demands the password of the specified *userid,* and if it is given, changes to that
*userid* and invokes the Shell sh(1) without changing the current directory or the user
environment (see environ(5)). The new user ID stays in force until the Shell exits.

If no *userid* is specified, `root` is assumed. To remind the super-user of his
responsibilities, the Shell substitutes `#` for its usual prompt.

**Note:** that prompt is chosen by sh(1) from the user id it finds itself running under, not
from the environment. *Su* also rewrites a **PS1** already in the environment, which on this
system there is not: login(1) exports HOME, PATH and TERM and nothing else.

**Note:** no password is demanded when the account has none, or when the caller is already the
super-user. The test is on the *real* user id, which is what makes it right in a program that
is set-user-id: by the time it runs, the effective id is already 0.

**Note:** *su* is set-user-id to root, and it is one of the two programs of this system for
which that bit exists to change an identity rather than to reach a directory operation.
login(1) is deliberately not set-user-id - its privilege is inherited from getty(8) - so before
*su* there was no way to change user from a shell at all.

**Note:** the change is one-way. setuid(2) here moves the real user id as well as the effective
one, so the shell *su* starts cannot climb back; leaving it is the only way out, and sh(1)
leaves on end-of-file rather than on **exit**, which merely reprompts an interactive shell.

## DIAGNOSTICS

Exit status is 1 for an unknown name, 2 for a wrong password and 3 if the shell cannot be
executed.

## SEE ALSO

sh(1), login(1), newgrp(1), passwd(1), setuid(2), crypt(3)
